## Enums with Custom Methods

An enum can have methods just like structs. Each variant can return different data through a method that uses `match`.

### The Mood Enum

```xiom
enum Mood {
  Happy,
  Sad,
  Excited,
  Tired,
  Hungry,
}
```

### Adding an Emoji Method

A method on the Mood type that returns an emoji string:

```xiom
fn Mood.emoji(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Happy => { return "\xF0\x9F\x98\x8A"; }
    Sad => { return "\xF0\x9F\x98\xA2"; }
    Excited => { return "\xF0\x9F\xA4\xA9"; }
    Tired => { return "\xF0\x9F\x98\xB4"; }
    Hungry => { return "\xF0\x9F\x8D\x94"; }
  }
}
```

`match` is how you branch on which variant `self` currently holds.

### Using Mood

```xiom
let current = Mood.Excited;
current.emoji();  // returns the emoji for Excited

let feeling = Mood.Hungry;
feeling.emoji();  // returns the emoji for Hungry
```

### Adding a Label Method

Each mood also has a text label:

```xiom
fn Mood.label(self) -> Str {
  match self {
    Happy => { return "Feeling great"; }
    Sad => { return "Feeling down"; }
    Excited => { return "Can't wait"; }
    Tired => { return "Need rest"; }
    Hungry => { return "Need food"; }
  }
}
```

### Method Chaining

```xiom
let mood = Mood.Happy;
let display = mood.emoji() + " " + mood.label();
```

